[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Isolation Levels and ANSI SQL Anomalies"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In relational database management systems, concurrency control is vital for maintaining data integrity while allowing multiple transactions to execute simultaneously. The ANSI/ISO SQL standard defines specific transaction isolation levels to balance consistency and performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Three Classic ANSI SQL Anomalies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before exploring isolation levels, we must understand the anomalies they are designed to prevent. These anomalies occur when concurrent transactions interact unsafely."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dirty Read: Occurs when a transaction reads data written by another concurrent uncommitted transaction. If the first transaction rolls back, the second has read invalid data."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Non-Repeatable Read: Occurs when a transaction reads the same row twice, but another concurrent transaction modifies or deletes that row and commits between the reads, resulting in different data."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phantom Read: Occurs when a transaction executes a query returning a set of rows satisfying a search condition, and a second concurrent transaction inserts new rows that match the condition and commits. The first transaction then re-runs the query and sees new 'phantom' rows."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"ANSI SQL Isolation Levels"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ANSI SQL-92 standard defines four isolation levels. Each level progressively restricts concurrent access to eliminate specific anomalies."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Uncommitted: Lowest isolation level. Allows dirty reads. Transactions can read uncommitted changes made by other transactions."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Committed: Prevents dirty reads. A transaction can only read data that has been committed before the read operation begins. Non-repeatable and phantom reads can still occur."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Repeatable Read: Prevents dirty reads and non-repeatable reads. If a transaction reads data, subsequent reads of the same data within the same transaction will yield the same results. Phantom reads may still occur."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serializable: The highest isolation level. Completely isolates transactions from one another, preventing dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads. It acts as if transactions are executed serially."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Isolation Levels in SQL"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can configure the isolation level for your database sessions using standard SQL commands. Below is an example of setting the transaction isolation level to Serializable in PostgreSQL."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;

-- Perform database operations
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 1;

COMMIT;"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the correct isolation level requires a trade-off between consistency and concurrency. Higher isolation levels like Serializable prevent all classic anomalies but can introduce performance bottlenecks due to lock contention or serialization failures, whereas lower levels increase throughput at the cost of data anomalies."}]
